// =============================================================================
//  probe_combat.hml — W4-3 acceptance probe for src/sim/combat.hml.
// =============================================================================
//
//  Run it FROM THE NIGHTSHADE REPO ROOT, and BOTH ways — the golden hashes
//  below are what make running it twice mean something:
//
//      hemlockc -O1 tools/probe_combat.hml -o /tmp/pcb && /tmp/pcb
//      hemlock  tools/probe_combat.hml
//
//  It exits 1 on any failure AND on an assertion-COUNT mismatch: a probe that
//  silently stops running half its checks is worse than no probe.
//
//  WHY THIS FILE CARRIES A WEAPON TABLE
//  -----------------------------------
//  `src/sim/weapons.hml` (W4-2) is a sibling task in the same sub-wave and does
//  not exist yet, so `combat.hml` takes its weapon numbers through four
//  injected functions (ARCHITECTURE §5.4b's injected-source pattern, the same
//  one `chunk.hml` uses for `worldgen.hml`).  The table below is the GDD §2.4
//  REFERENCE, transcribed here in the TEST, deliberately NOT in the sim module,
//  so that combat.hml contains no shadow copy of someone else's data.  When
//  weapons.hml lands, these four functions are replaced by four one-line
//  forwards and every assertion below should still pass unchanged.
//
//  Longshadow falloff is 34/68, not GDD's 55/110 — ARCHITECTURE §0 D2.
//
//  TIMING (CLAUDE.md §1.2 / RULE T)
//  --------------------------------
//  `__clock()` CPU time, minimum of 7 whole batches.  Bounds are REGRESSION
//  GUARDS at ~2x the LOADED measurement on a box permanently sharing ~17 of 24
//  cores; both numbers are printed in the assertion text.
// =============================================================================

